research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-thm-completely-normal-iff-hereditarily-normal.result.json:16:  "tail": "VERDICT: CLEAN\n\nBOUNDARY: Both directions of the biconditional were verified independently.  \nForward direction (1.1): If X is completely normal, any subspace S (including empty and singleton) is normal. Disjoint closed A,B in S are separated in S by the definition of separated sets applied to the subspace, hence separated in X by the absoluteness property of separation, so complete normality yields ambient disjoint open sets whose traces give disjoint open sets in S.  \nReverse direction (1.2‑4.1): Assume every subspace of X is normal and let A,B be separated in X. Define Y = X \\ ((cl A \\ A) ∪ (cl B \\ B)). The cases where A or B (or both) are empty are handled without failure: if A=∅, then Y = X \\ (cl B \\ B), B is closed in Y, normality of Y supplies a separating open set for B, and the empty set is trivially separated; the constructed G′,H′ still work. If both are empty, Y = X and the argument reduces to normality of X. For non‑empty separated sets, the construction ensures A,B ⊆ Y and they are disjoint closed in Y. Normality of Y gives disjoint open U,V in Y, traced from open G,H in X. The key containment G∩H ⊆ (cl A \\ A) ∪ (cl B \\ B) follows from U∩V=∅. Then G \\ cl B and H \\ cl A are disjoint open sets in X containing A,B respectively, because any point in their intersection would lie in G∩H but outside cl A ∪ cl B, contradicting the containment. The argument uses no choice, no extra separation axioms, and correctly handles all subspace topologies.\n\nSOURCES: All five supplied cited items were inspected.  \ndef-completely-normal-and-perfectly-normal-spaces (F1): defines completely normal exactly as used in steps 1.1 and 4.1.  \ndef-normal-and-t4-spaces (F1): defines normal exactly as used in step 1.1 and for the normality of Y in step 2.1.  \ndef-separated-sets (F2): contains the definition of separated sets, the fact that disjoint closed sets are separated, and the absolute property (separation in a subspace iff separation in the ambient space). These are correctly applied in steps 1.1, 1.2, and 3.1.  \ndef-subspace-topology-top (F3): states that open sets in a subspace are traces of open sets in the ambient space, used in steps 1.1 and 2.1.  \ndef-hereditary-property is declared as a dependency but is not load‑bearing in the proof; it does not affect mathematical correctness.  \nEvery citation is materially accurate and supports the exact use.\n\nCHECKED: The title, the public claim (“A space is completely normal if and only if every one of its subspaces is normal. Equivalently, complete normality is exactly hereditary normality.”), every numbered proof step (1.1, 1.2, 2.1, 3.1, 4.1), and the Remarks (none present) were read and verified."
